# dodai.app — Cursor project rules

These rules are mandatory for every change in the dodai.app codebase.
Treat them as product and engineering constraints, not as optional suggestions.

## 0. Source of truth and decision hierarchy

Before writing code, inspect the repository and read the current project documents if they exist:

- `docs/dodai-product-definition-v1.html` — product definition and business rules.
- `docs/dodai-design-system-v1.html` — visual system and component rules.
- `docs/dodai-app-product-and-implementation-plan.docx` — architecture and MVP boundaries.
- `docs/adr/` — accepted architecture decisions.

If paths differ, locate the equivalent files. Do not silently create a second source of truth.

When documents conflict, use this order:

1. Explicit user requirement in the current task.
2. Accepted Architecture Decision Record (ADR).
3. Product definition.
4. Design system.
5. Existing implementation.
6. Cursor's own assumption.

If a request conflicts with an invariant below, stop and explain the conflict before coding.
Do not solve an ambiguous product decision by inventing a feature.

## 1. Product invariants

These rules must never be violated accidentally:

- Product name: `dodai.app`. Brand shorthand: `dodai`. Visual lockup: `dodai+`.
- dodai.app is a catalog and discovery platform for Ukrainian digital products.
- The catalog/search experience is the main product. The map is context and secondary navigation.
- A product is Ukrainian when it has Ukrainian founders or a significant Ukrainian team; this must be reviewable by moderation.
- Basic placement is free (short card) or Passport at 300 UAH one-time (full passport). Paid options never buy organic rank or dofollow; outbound stays sponsored/nofollow.
- Map placement and promotional placements are separate paid options.
- Paid placement never changes organic votes or organic ranking.
- `Product of the Week` cannot be bought. It is editorially selected and/or voted on under the documented launch rules.
- Votes are independent of payment and cannot be sold, bundled or increased by a paid plan.
- Comments are not part of MVP. Do not add a comments database table, UI or API unless the product decision explicitly reopens this.
- Do not sell dofollow links. Paid outbound links must use `rel="sponsored nofollow noopener"`; user-generated links use `rel="ugc nofollow noopener"`.
- Paid placements must be visually separated and explicitly labelled (`Promoted` or the approved Ukrainian equivalent).
- The platform must remain useful without an outbound click: product description, platforms, pricing model, language, screenshots and update information belong on the public page.
- Do not copy AppMap's code, database, design or money-based ranking. A product's visibility is not the same thing as product quality.

## 2. Working mode for Cursor

Before modifying code:

1. Inspect the relevant directory, route, component and data flow.
2. Identify the product rule, design token and API boundary affected.
3. Write a short implementation plan in the response before making a non-trivial change.
4. Reuse existing components and patterns before creating new ones.
5. Keep the change narrow. Do not refactor unrelated code in the same task.

After modifying code:

1. Run the relevant type check, lint, unit tests and build checks.
2. Test loading, empty, error, success, disabled and responsive states where applicable.
3. Check keyboard navigation and visible focus.
4. Report changed files, validation performed and any unresolved risk.

Never claim a feature is complete when it was only mocked in the UI.
Never hide a failing test, TypeScript error, migration issue or build warning.

## 3. Architecture and boundaries

Use the agreed stack unless a written ADR changes it (see `docs/adr/001-stack-vercel-neon.md`):

- Next.js App Router and TypeScript.
- Vercel deployment.
- Neon Postgres with Drizzle ORM and server-side access patterns.
- Auth.js magic-link authentication (Resend).
- Vercel Blob for product media; Vercel Cron for scheduled jobs.
- PaymentProvider adapter for WayForPay and/or Monobank.
- PostHog and Google Analytics for product analytics where approved.
- Zod (or the repository's approved validation library) at every external boundary.

Keep boundaries explicit:

- `app/` contains routes, layouts, metadata and route-level composition.
- `components/ui/` contains reusable visual primitives only.
- `components/domain/` contains reusable product components such as ProductCard, VoteControl and PlacementBadge.
- `features/` contains user journeys such as catalog search, submit product, voting and placements.
- `lib/` contains pure utilities, tokens, validation, analytics and shared types.
- `server/` or route handlers contain database, payment and privileged operations.
- UI components must not call the database directly unless the repository has an explicit approved client boundary.
- Domain components must not contain payment, moderation or authorization decisions.
- Server code must enforce every permission; hiding a button is not authorization.
- Keep business logic out of JSX. Put it in named functions, services or domain modules that can be tested.
- Do not introduce global state for data that belongs to a route or feature.
- Prefer server components for read-only SEO content and client components only for interaction.
- Keep client bundles small; do not make an entire page client-side to support one button.

## 4. Component-first implementation

The design system is the only approved source for interface decisions.

Use existing primitives for:

- Button, LinkButton, IconButton.
- Input, SearchField, Select, Checkbox and FormField.
- Card, ProductCard, ProductRow and ProductGrid.
- Badge, StatusBadge, PlacementBadge and PlatformTag.
- Modal, Drawer, Toast, Tooltip and ConfirmDialog.
- EmptyState, ErrorState, Skeleton and Pagination.
- VoteControl, CompactProductOfDay, MapMarker and MapLegend.

If a needed primitive does not exist:

1. Create it in the shared UI layer.
2. Give it a clear API and documented states.
3. Add a usage example or test.
4. Compose it into the feature.

Never duplicate a component by copying JSX into a second screen.
Never create a one-off visual pattern when an existing component can be composed.
Do not build a separate component for a single text wrapper or one-time layout unless it has a clear semantic role.

Every interactive component must support:

- default;
- hover;
- focus-visible;
- pressed/active;
- disabled;
- loading;
- error where relevant;
- keyboard operation;
- mobile layout.

## 5. Design-system rules

Use design tokens. Do not introduce arbitrary values in feature code.

### Color

Use semantic tokens, not raw hex values:

- `ink`: `#141616`
- `canvas`: `#F6F4EF`
- `surface`: `#FFFDFA`
- `copper`: `#C76B4C`
- `copper-dark`: `#944B38`
- product surfaces: `peach`, `mint`, `lilac`, `sky`
- semantic colors: `success`, `warning`, `danger`, `info`

Copper is the brand/action signal. Do not use it as the only indication of an error, success or payment status.
Product surfaces provide context; they do not replace semantic states.

### Typography

- Use Geologica for headings, UI labels and product names.
- Use IBM Plex Mono only for metadata, categories, IDs, dates and technical labels.
- Do not introduce another font without an ADR.
- Keep the established scale and line-height tokens.
- Do not use all-caps for long user-facing copy.

### Spacing, shape and elevation

- Use the 8px spacing rhythm: 8, 12, 16, 24, 32, 48, 72.
- Use 16px radius for standard cards and fields.
- Use pill shapes only for actions, filters and status badges.
- Use 1px borders for structure.
- Use soft shadows sparingly; no glassmorphism, hard 3D cards or decorative gradients in every component.
- Product cards must feel like product passports/index cards, not credit cards.

### Logo

- Primary lockup: `dodai` + copper add module.
- Compact mark: `d+` only for favicon, app icon, map marker and very small UI surfaces.
- Respect clear space equal to the height of the `+` module.
- Never stretch, rotate, recolor, add shadows to or redraw the logo locally.
- The `+` is a meaningful add action, not a random decoration.

## 6. Product-card rules

Every product card must answer these questions quickly:

1. What is the product called?
2. What problem does it solve?
3. Who is it for or where does it work?
4. What can I do next?

Required structure:

- product icon or approved fallback;
- product name;
- one-sentence value description;
- category and platform metadata;
- explicit action such as `Відкрити`;
- vote control where voting is available;
- commercial or editorial status badge when applicable.

Rules:

- Keep descriptions to one useful sentence; do not use vague marketing claims.
- `Product of the Week` is an editorial badge and must never be shown on a paid placement.
- `Promoted` is a commercial badge and must remain visible in every layout.
- Organic lists and sponsored lists are separate data blocks, not one array sorted by payment.
- Do not rank a product by the amount paid.
- Do not use color alone to communicate product status.

## 7. Search, catalog and map

- Search is task-first: support product name, problem, category, platform and relevant tags.
- Always implement loading, empty-result, error and clear-search states.
- Record zero-result searches for future taxonomy work.
- Filters must be URL-addressable and shareable.
- Keep organic sorting separate from paid slots.
- The map is an exploration layer, not the primary ranking.
- Use clusters at scale; do not render hundreds of overlapping markers.
- Map markers use compact `d+` for dodai context and product initials/logo for products.
- Never make marker size depend on payment *amount* (no pay-more → continuously bigger). Map uses three discrete tiers: (1) free small logo at dot size, (2) paid mid logo up to 25 concurrent, (3) paid large logo up to 5 concurrent. Size tiers by arbitrary spend beyond these SKUs are forbidden.
- The map must have a list or accessible alternative view.

## 8. Add-product flow

The canonical flow is URL-first:

1. Accept a website, App Store or Google Play URL without requiring registration.
2. Fetch and preview title, description, Open Graph image, favicon and domain safely.
3. Let the user correct metadata.
4. Collect category, platforms, payment model, Ukrainian-language availability, city and Ukrainian connection.
5. Collect owner email and send a magic link.
6. Show free vs Passport (300 UAH) listing terms and optional paid placements from `/pricing`.
7. Create a payment intent/order with an idempotency key.
8. Confirm payment only through the provider webhook.
9. Send the submission to moderation.
10. Publish only after approval; otherwise record a reason and process the documented refund path.

Do not allow a client-side success screen to activate a product, placement or badge.

## 9. Payments, placements and moderation

- Webhook confirmation is the only source of truth for paid status.
- Every payment handler must be idempotent.
- A paid slot is a time-bounded inventory record with `starts_at`, `ends_at`, surface and status.
- Prevent overlapping reservations transactionally.
- Product publication always passes moderation, including when payment succeeded.
- Owners cannot edit moderation status, votes, awards, payment status or placement dates directly.
- Store admin actions in an audit log.
- Do not promise traffic, sales, rankings or dofollow SEO value in commercial copy.
- Paid placements must state their duration and surface before payment.
- Base placement has no automatic claim of `Verified`; verification requires a separate review process.

## 10. Links, SEO, GEO and outbound tracking

- Use an allowlist for outbound destinations and protect every redirect from open redirect attacks.
- Preferred paid link attributes: `rel="sponsored nofollow noopener"`.
- Preferred user-generated link attributes: `rel="ugc nofollow noopener"`.
- Never create paid dofollow links.
- Redirect endpoints must be `noindex` and disallowed from crawling where appropriate.
- Public product pages are indexable only when active and moderated.
- Implement canonical URL, title, description, Open Graph, sitemap, robots and BreadcrumbList.
- Use SoftwareApplication or WebApplication structured data only when the page has the required facts.
- Do not generate thin SEO pages for empty categories, unmoderated submissions or expired placements.
- Track outbound clicks without sending private comments, nicknames or unnecessary personal data to analytics.
- Follow `docs/adr/003-seo-geo-foundations.md`: keep `llms.txt`, citable product facts, AI crawler allowlist and GEO fields (`seoTitle`, `seoDescription`, `ukraineNote`, `lastVerifiedAt`, `sameAs`) in sync with public pages.
- Filter query URLs on `/products` must be `noindex`; category pages index only with intro + enough products.
- Do not invent GEO claims (guaranteed AI citations, sold dofollow, pay-to-rank).

## 11. Data, validation and security

- Validate all external input at the boundary with Zod or the approved validator.
- Normalize domains and prevent duplicate active products by domain or store ID.
- Sanitize and constrain user-provided text; never render raw HTML from submissions.
- Accept only approved URL schemes and destinations.
- Validate image MIME type, dimensions and size; strip EXIF where the pipeline supports it.
- Use server-side authorization and database constraints; never trust a client role or hidden field.
- Rate-limit submissions, votes, magic links and redirect events.
- Use Turnstile or the approved anti-bot layer on public abuse-prone actions.
- Keep secrets server-side and never expose service-role keys to the browser.
- Use database migrations for schema changes. Do not edit production schema manually.
- Add unique constraints for normalized domain, slug, payment provider ID, idempotency key, vote per launch and non-overlapping placement slots.

## 12. Analytics and observability

Use the approved event names and do not invent variants casually:

- `search_submitted`
- `filter_applied`
- `product_impression`
- `product_opened`
- `outbound_clicked`
- `submit_started`
- `metadata_loaded`
- `submit_completed`
- `payment_started`
- `payment_succeeded`
- `moderation_approved`
- `vote_started`
- `vote_cast`
- `placement_viewed`
- `placement_clicked`
- `badge_copied`
- `share_card_downloaded`

Events must have a documented payload, a stable product ID and an attribution context where relevant.
Do not send raw user text, private emails, comments or unnecessary personal data to analytics.

## 13. Accessibility and responsive behavior

- Every control must have an accessible name.
- Minimum interactive target: 44×44px.
- Visible `:focus-visible` state is mandatory.
- Maintain readable contrast on canvas, product surfaces and copper backgrounds.
- Never communicate status by color alone.
- Provide a list/accessibility alternative for the map.
- Test at keyboard-only, mobile width and 200% zoom.
- Keep all essential content available without hover.
- Use responsive composition, not simply smaller desktop components.

## 14. Testing requirements

For every feature, add the smallest appropriate set of:

- unit tests for pure logic;
- component tests for important states;
- integration tests for form/payment/moderation boundaries;
- E2E tests for critical user journeys.

At minimum, preserve tests for:

- URL metadata loading and correction;
- duplicate product detection;
- payment webhook idempotency;
- moderation approval and rejection/refund;
- one vote per user per launch;
- Product of the Week independence from payment;
- sponsored/nofollow link attributes;
- expired placements disappearing automatically;
- safe redirect allowlist;
- accessible search, cards and map alternative.

## 15. File and naming conventions

- Use TypeScript strict mode.
- Use `PascalCase` for React components and `camelCase` for functions/variables.
- Use kebab-case for route segments and file names where the repository convention allows it.
- Prefer named exports for reusable components.
- Keep types close to the domain that owns them; shared types go in the approved shared types module.
- Do not create `utils.ts`, `helpers.ts` or `misc.ts` as a dumping ground.
- Keep one responsibility per module.
- Every new environment variable must be documented and validated at startup.
- Every schema or migration change needs a migration file and a short ADR when it affects a boundary.

## 16. Definition of Done

A change is complete only when:

- It follows the product invariants.
- It uses existing design-system tokens and components.
- It has no arbitrary hardcoded colors, spacing, radii or duplicated component markup.
- It handles loading, empty, error, success, disabled and mobile states where relevant.
- It is authorized server-side and validated at external boundaries.
- It includes analytics only from the approved vocabulary.
- It has tests appropriate to its risk.
- It passes typecheck, lint, tests and build.
- SEO and accessibility are checked for public pages.
- The final response names changed files, checks run and any remaining risk.

## 17. Forbidden shortcuts

Do not:

- hardcode a visual fix in one page instead of updating the component or token;
- add inline styles to bypass the design system;
- duplicate a ProductCard or Button with slightly different markup;
- put payment, vote or moderation rules only in the client;
- activate a product after a client-side payment response;
- mix sponsored products into organic sorting;
- sell or emit dofollow links;
- add comments, recommendations, personalization or mobile apps to MVP without an explicit product decision;
- use map bubbles as a payment-based ranking;
- add a dependency for a problem solved by the existing stack;
- rewrite unrelated files during a focused task;
- suppress errors or claim successful validation without running it.

When a shortcut appears necessary, stop and propose the smallest reusable abstraction or an ADR.
